8
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Brenda Song (born March 27, 1988) is an American actress.

9
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
Born in Carmichael, California, Song began her career at
the age of six, working as a child model.

10
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:06,000
She made her screen debut with a guest appearance on the sitcom Thunder Alley (1995), and went on to roles such as the
children's television series Fudge (1995) and the Nickelodeon series 100 Deeds for Eddie McDowd (1999).

11
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:06,000
She starred in the Disney Channel original film The Ultimate Christmas
Present (2000), which won her a Young Artist Award.

12
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
She subsequently signed a contract with Disney Channel and earned widespread recognition for playing the titular character in the action film Wendy Wu:
Homecoming Warrior (2006), and London Tipton in The Suite Life franchise (2005–2011), earning her acclaim and two Young Hollywood Awards.

13
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
She additionally played the recurring role of Tia in Phil of the Future (2004–2005), and had starring roles in the television
film Get a Clue (2002), the sports comedy film Like Mike (2002) and the comedy film Stuck in the Suburbs (2004).

14
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Song made her transition into mainstream roles with the critically acclaimed biographical drama film The Social Network (2010) and
went on to roles in the ABC political thriller Scandal (2012–2013), the Fox sitcom New Girl and the sitcom Dads (2013).

15
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
In October 2014, she signed a talent holding deal with Fox and 20th Century Fox Television and was subsequently cast in several television pilots
for NBC and CBS, including the medical drama series Pure Genius (2016–2017) and the action drama series Station 19 (2018–2020).

16
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
She returned to Disney Channel to provide the voice of Anne Boonchuy in the animated series Amphibia (2019–2022), starred as Madison Maxwell in
the Hulu comedy-drama series Dollface (2019–2022), and provides the voice of Princess Akemi in Blue Eye Samurai (2023–present).

17
01:25:00,000 --> 01:25:06,000
She also appeared in the romantic comedy Angry Angel (2017), the psychological thriller Secret Obsession (2019), the
comedy-drama Changeland (2019), the romantic comedy Love Accidentally and the horror video-game The Quarry (both 2022).

18
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Song earned renewed recognition for starring as a showgirl in the drama film The Last Showgirl
(2024) and a chief of staff in the Netflix series Running Point (2025–present).
